<h1 class="visually-hidden">Cyberpedia</h1>
<p class="standfirst">Sound design &amp; implementation <span class="ph">&middot; [2025] &middot; [team or studio name]</span></p>

<h2>Project overview</h2>
<!-- PLACEHOLDER -->
<div class="ph">
  <p>Cyberpedia is a narrative exploration game set inside a sprawling, half-corrupted online encyclopaedia. Players restore damaged articles to uncover what happened to the people who wrote them, moving between clean, orderly pages and glitching, broken ones.</p>
  <p>The world needed to sound like the inside of a machine without feeling cold: part dial-up modem, part library, part half-remembered dream.</p>
</div>

<h2>My role</h2>
<!-- PLACEHOLDER -->
<div class="ph">
  <ul>
    <li>Lead sound designer: all sound effects, UI and ambience ([120+] assets)</li>
    <li>Audio implementation in FMOD Studio and Unity</li>
    <li>Mixing and loudness for the final build</li>
    <li>Music integration (music by [composer])</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2>Technical approach</h2>
<!-- PLACEHOLDER -->
<div class="ph">
  <p>Every ambience was built in two versions, clean and corrupted, and crossfaded in FMOD by a single <i>corruption</i> parameter driven by the game. As an article is restored the glitches thin out and the room tone settles, so the player hears their progress before they see it.</p>
  <p>UI sounds use multi-instruments with randomised pitch and round-robin selection, so repeated typing never sounds mechanical, and mixer snapshots duck the world under dialogue and menus.</p>
</div>

